PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       BSD _c_u_r_s_e_s has no _p_a_d feature.

       SVr2 _c_u_r_s_e_s (1986) provided the nneewwppaadd and related functions, document‐
       ing  them  in  a single line each.  SVr3 (1987) provided more extensive
       documentation.

       The documentation does not explain the term _p_a_d.  However,  the  Apollo
       _A_e_g_i_s workstation operating system supported a graphical _p_a_d feature:

       •   These  graphical pads could be much larger than the computer's dis‐
           play.

       •   The read-only output from a command could be scrolled back  to  in‐
           spect, and select text from the pad.

       The two uses may be related.

       X/Open  Curses,  Issue 4 describes these functions, without significant
       change from the SVr3 documentation.  It describes no error  conditions.
       The  behavior  of  ssuubbppaadd  if the parent window is not a pad is undocu‐
       mented, and is not checked by the vendor Unix implementations:

       •   SVr4 _c_u_r_s_e_s sets a flag in the _W_I_N_D_O_W  structure  in  nneewwppaadd  which
           tells if the window is a _p_a_d.

           However,  it  uses this information only in wwaaddddcchh (to decide if it
           should call wwrreeffrreesshh) and wwssccrrll (to avoid  scrolling  a  pad),  and
           does  not  check  in  wwrreeffrreesshh  to ensure that the pad is refreshed
           properly.

       •   Solaris _x_c_u_r_s_e_s checks whether a window is a pad  in  wwnnoouuttrreeffrreesshh,
           returning EERRRR in that case.

           However,  it only sets the flag for subwindows if the parent window
           is a pad.  Its nneewwppaadd function does not set this information.  Con‐
           sequently, the check will never fail.

           It  makes no comparable check in ppnnoouuttrreeffrreesshh, though interestingly
           enough, a comment in the source code states  that  the  lack  of  a
           check was an MKS extension.

       •   NetBSD  7 _c_u_r_s_e_s sets a flag in the _W_I_N_D_O_W structure for nneewwppaadd and
           ssuubbppaadd,  using  this  to  help   with   the   distinction   between
           wwnnoouuttrreeffrreesshh and ppnnoouuttrreeffrreesshh.

           It  does  not  check for the case where a subwindow is created in a
           pad using ssuubbwwiinn or ddeerrwwiinn.

           The dduuppwwiinn function returns a regular  window  when  duplicating  a
           pad.   Likewise,  ggeettwwiinn always returns a window, even if the saved
           data was from a pad.

       This implementation

       •   sets a flag in the _W_I_N_D_O_W structure for nneewwppaadd and ssuubbppaadd,

       •   allows a ssuubbwwiinn or ddeerrwwiinn call to succeed having a  pad  parent  by
           forcing the subwindow to be a pad,

       •   checks  in  both  wwnnoouuttrreeffrreesshh and ppnnoouuttrreeffrreesshh to ensure that pads
           and windows are handled distinctly, and

       •   ensures that dduuppwwiinn and ggeettwwiinn treat pads  versus  windows  consis‐
           tently.

